</figure>
<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#A4.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Appendix D Additional Data Analysis ‣ Humor in Pixels: Benchmarking Large Multimodal Models Understanding of Online Comics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and <a href="#A4.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Appendix D Additional Data Analysis ‣ Humor in Pixels: Benchmarking Large Multimodal Models Understanding of Online Comics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> present the statistical distributions of panels and words in the <span id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">PixelHumor</span> dataset, respectively. The dataset includes highly complex comics, with some featuring up to 18 panels and 501 words, as observed in <span id="A4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SMBC</span>. On average, comics from <span id="A4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Peanuts</span> and <span id="A4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Happiness and Cyanide</span> tend to have a higher number of panels, while those from <span id="A4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PhD Comics</span> and <span id="A4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SMBC</span> are notably more text-heavy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A4.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ Appendix D Additional Data Analysis ‣ Humor in Pixels: Benchmarking Large Multimodal Models Understanding of Online Comics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> provides a breakdown of the <span id="A4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">PixelHumor</span> dataset by data source and humor styles, showcasing the diversity of humor types represented across the various sources.</p>
</div>
